      "html": "<p>The Care Inspectorate uses its own vocabulary, and English-system terms do not map onto it cleanly. These are the words that matter when you self-evaluate a Scottish service. Always work from the current published framework and standards for the authoritative definitions.</p><dl><dt>Quality framework</dt><dd>The Care Inspectorate's framework for evaluating a care service. It organises the evaluation into key questions and quality indicators, and services grade themselves against it. Different sectors have their own version, such as How good is our early learning and childcare? for nurseries.</dd><dt>Key questions</dt><dd>The high-level questions the quality framework is built around, such as how good the care and support, the setting, the staff team and the leadership are. Each key question is graded.</dd><dt>Quality indicators</dt><dd>The more detailed descriptions of strong practice that sit under each key question. They set out what good looks like and are what a service gathers evidence against.</dd><dt>Six-point grade scale</dt><dd>The scale on which each key question is graded, from unsatisfactory at the bottom to excellent at the top. It runs 1 to 6, where 1 is unsatisfactory and 6 is excellent.</dd><dt>Self-evaluation</dt><dd>The ongoing, honest process by which a service judges its own quality against the quality framework and the standards, and evidences each judgement. It is an expectation, not a one-off form, and it does not raise a grade on its own.</dd><dt>Improvement plan</dt><dd>The live plan that turns each gap the self-evaluation finds into an action with an owner and a date. An inspector checks whether self-evaluation is honest and matched by action, so the plan is where a candid evaluation earns its keep.</dd><dt>Health and Social Care Standards</dt><dd>The national standards that set out what people should expect from their care and support in Scotland. They are used alongside the quality framework as the basis for both self-evaluation and inspection.</dd></dl><p>Quickler captures self-evaluation and evidence against this vocabulary. It is not a care record, eMAR, care management or clinical system, and holds no resident care data.</p>"
